.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-2-Clause
.Dd September 6, 2026
.Dt SYS-INIT-BUILD-NATIVE-SUPERVISOR 1
.Os sys-init experimental
.Sh NAME
.Nm sys-init-build-native-supervisor
.Nd build an inert experimental Aether artifact from local inputs
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm python3
.Ar scripts/build-native-supervisor.py
.Ar native output
.Op Fl Fl variant Ar name
.Nm python3 scripts/build-native-supervisor.py
.Fl Fl help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
This source-checkout developer tool builds files only; it does not boot a
guest,
install a service or execute the generated image.
Use Python 3.10 or newer on a
POSIX development host, from the repository root.
No root privileges are needed.
A successful build establishes the recorded structural checks, not native
runtime
acceptance.
Use the separately qualified runner and matching producer profile
before making a target-behavior claim.
.Pp
Provision rustc through rustup with toolchain 1.98.0 and the
x86_64-unknown-none target before offline use.
The command invokes rustc +1.98.0
with edition 2024, optimization level 2, aborting panics, static relocation
and
the repository linker script.
It requires nonempty raw code at most 4096 bytes.
The bounds belong to these legacy fixtures, not the newer init profiles or a
production memory budget.
The builder does not provision a toolchain itself.
The input must be longer than 40 bytes and begin with AENATC01.
This is only
an initial envelope check; the builder does not fully validate its declared
fields, authenticate its source or prove variant/input compatibility.
It binds
the input payload to the selected supervisor under AENSUP01 or AENFD001.
.Pp
Each compiler/tool invocation has a 180-second deadline and a 4 MiB
merged-output
limit.
Failure kills only the owned group of trusted, non-detaching tools and
retains the accepted log prefix.
The command is not confinement against hostile
tools or concurrent input mutation.
Copying, hashing and other Python work are
outside this per-tool deadline.
.Sh OPTIONS AND OPERANDS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ar native
Path to the reviewed native C envelope.
This input is read, not executed.
.It Ar output
Output directory, created with parents as needed.
Existing directories are
allowed and files with the same names can be overwritten.
Use a fresh private
build directory.
After failure, earlier artifacts may still exist; their presence
is not proof that the latest build succeeded.
.It Fl Fl variant Ar name
Select a fixed fixture variant; the default is pass.
Choices are listed below.
.It Fl Fl help
Print usage and exit without building or creating output.
.El
.Pp
There is no --version option.
Invalid usage exits 2.
.Sh VARIANTS
.Bl -item -compact
.It
pass
.It
fault_entry
.It
wrong_detail
.It
io
.It
io-stop
.It
io-stop-wrong-detail
.It
io-retained-stop
.It
io-retained-fault
.It
io-retained-wrong-kind
.It
io-ready-exit
.It
io-ready-fault
.It
io-unready-exit
.It
io-unready-fault
.It
io-unready-stop
.It
io-invent-ready
.It
io-authority
.It
io-authority-allow
.It
io-peer
.It
io-peer-reverse
.It
io-peer-wrong
.El
.Pp
Names select fixed fixture paths and deliberate fault/rejection controls.
They do not establish that an arbitrary native input is suitable for that
path.
.Sh OUTPUT AND FILES
The successful command prints an artifact path on standard output.
Compiler/linker output is retained in logs.
Python and argument errors use
standard error and may include tracebacks.
Preserve the entire output directory,
including failed attempts; a running/missing receipt is not a successful
build.
Output includes supervisor.raw, supervised.bin, build.json, compiler.log and
compiler.log.json. build.json records the command, variant and
input/raw/envelope
hashes.
The per-tool receipt additionally records caller/helper hashes and the
bounded invocation outcome; it does not replace build.json.
.Pp
A post-compile size or structural failure can follow a successful tool
attempt.
Check the builder exit and final evidence, not just the compiler receipt.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
The compiler inherits the parent environment.
PATH and rustup configuration
select the rustc proxy/toolchain.
The shared scripts/bounded_tools.py helper is
a host build dependency; its digest and the caller digest appear in tool
receipts.
Provision dependencies separately so offline builds cannot trigger setup work.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It 0
The build and its final checks completed; also supported help/version queries.
.It 1
An explicit build rejection or ordinary uncaught tool/Python failure.
Signal or abrupt termination can produce other statuses without final
evidence.
.It 2
Invalid argparse usage, including --version.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
From the repository root, inspect usage without launching tools:
.Bd -literal -offset left
python3 scripts/build-native-supervisor.py --help
.Ed
.Pp
After setting NATIVE to a reviewed local envelope path, choose a fresh
directory:
.Bd -literal -offset left
python3 scripts/build-native-supervisor.py \e
  "$NATIVE" out/supervisor-review --variant pass
.Ed
.Pp
Expected success is an artifact path and exit zero.
These examples do not boot
or execute the artifact.
Do not reuse paths containing retained acceptance data.
.Sh SEE ALSO
Repository guides: docs/NATIVE-SUPERVISION.md, docs/QUALITY-PROCESS.md,
docs/DOCUMENTATION-INVENTORY.md and the source script for exact build flags.
.Sh CAVEATS
The manual title is not an installed command.
Independent technical/reader
review and target manual lookup remain pending.
Build success is not runtime
conformance or authority to publish a release.
